# the-bubble-boundary — plan 11 F5b.
#
# The first scenario in dryopea that starts OUTSIDE the core's
# scrambler bubble.  Every earlier one spawns twelve hexes out and the
# bubble is twenty-five, so all of them were already inside it — which
# is why none of them could see this phase.
#
# ── The handoff, stated as coordinates ──────────────────────────
#
# The spawn heading here is 4, which is (-1, 0): it changes q and
# NEVER r.  So:
#
#   * `enemy 0 26 0` after four ticks — still on the heading's line,
#     one hex OUTSIDE the boundary.  The field would have sent it to
#     (26, -1) from there; it did not go, because it cannot hear the
#     core yet.
#   * `enemy 0 25 -1` two ticks later — r has changed, so the FIELD
#     is steering.  That is the handoff, and it happened at exactly
#     the hex the radius names.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   r =  0 :  (25,0) … (30,0)     the approach leg
#   r = -1 :  (0,-1) … (26,-1)    the route home
#   (0,0)  :  the core
#   (24,0) :  deliberately NOT painted
#
# That last line is what makes a broken handoff loud rather than
# subtle: an enemy that never switches mode walks its heading into the
# dead end at (25, 0) and stands there for the rest of the run.

echo the bubble boundary: five hexes of heading, then the field takes over

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, THIRTY hexes out — five clear of the bubble, so the run
# gets to watch approach mode work before the handoff.
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 30 0
marker 30 0 spawn 4

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

# The approach leg, the route home, and the core hex itself.  The
# route home is a SEPARATE row: it is what the field will steer along,
# and the leg does not reach it except at (25, 0).
palette 5
drag 30 0 25 0
drag 26 -1 0 -1
click 0 0

count painted 34 34
kind 25 0 grass
kind 24 0 sea

wave 3
count alive 3 3
enemy 0 30 0
enemies passable
range 30 30
snap bubble-wave-starts

# Four ticks of approach — still outside, still on r = 0.  The field
# would have turned it north at (26, 0); it kept its heading.
tick 4
enemy 0 26 0
range 26 26
enemies passable

# One more puts it exactly on the boundary hex.
tick 1
enemy 0 25 0
range 25 25

# And the next step is the FIELD's: r changes, which this heading
# cannot do.  The handoff, at the hex the radius names and not before.
tick 1
enemy 0 25 -1
enemies passable
range 25 25
snap bubble-handoff

# Twenty-five more and they are home.
tick 25
enemy 0 0 0
enemy 2 0 0
enemies passable
range 0 0
count alive 3 3
